What really happens in your mind when you're alone?

This video explores the psychology of being alone, from the difference between solitude and loneliness to the way social disconnection can affect the brain and body. We look at attachment, social pain, the default mode network, rumination, creativity, mindfulness, and why chosen solitude can sometimes be deeply restorative.

Settle in and explore what psychology and neuroscience reveal about loneliness, solitude, and learning to feel comfortable in your own company.

